WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.980
From a Viking revenge tale to the&nbsp;
true story of a boxing legend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:09.360
these films are worth checking out&nbsp;
even though they didn't make much cash.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:14.100
Although he's been one of Hollywood's top&nbsp;
screenwriters ever since he penned "Lethal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:20.400
Weapon" in 1987, Shane Black didn't make his&nbsp;
directorial debut until 2005 with "Kiss Kiss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.800
Bang Bang." His hilarious noir flavored&nbsp;
comedy with bursts of action proved that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:31.080
Black knew what he was doing behind the camera.&nbsp;
After directing "Iron Man 3," Black returned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:35.460
with another noir-action-comedy&nbsp;
hybrid in 2016: "The Nice Guys."

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:42.360
With a clever script, a fun '70s LA setting,&nbsp;
creative action sequences, and excellent chemistry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:48.780
between stars Russell Crowe and Ryan Gosling,&nbsp;
"The Nice Guys" is easy to love. What wasn't easy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:53.100
on the other hand, was making back its&nbsp;
$50 million budget at the box office.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.420
Though much larger audiences eventually&nbsp;
came around to the film once it hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:03.240
streaming services, "The Nice Guys" only&nbsp;
brought in $36 million domestically — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:07.920
massive drop-off from Black's over&nbsp;
$1.2 billion-earning MCU endeavor.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:09.540
"Y'know, nobody got hurt."

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.400
"A few people got hurt."

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.040
"I'm saying I think they died quickly,&nbsp;
though, so I don't think that they got hurt."

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:19.260
Black returned to franchise filmmaking with his&nbsp;
next outing in the form of "The Predator," but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:23.820
again failed to make back the film's budget&nbsp;
at the domestic box office once again.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:29.880
Peter Strickland isn't a name that comes up often&nbsp;
when the best horror directors of the 21st century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:35.580
are being discussed, but maybe that ought to&nbsp;
change. Much like Strickland's earlier film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:41.820
"Berberian Sound Studio" and later film "Flux&nbsp;
Gourmet," "In Fabric" is a stylish good time.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:45.240
Rather than slotting into the&nbsp;
current trends of modern horror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:51.060
"In Fabric" instead looks to the past. The film is&nbsp;
styled like an Italian Giallo film from the '70s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:56.460
and uses that aesthetic to present the story&nbsp;
of a haunted dress as it exchanges unfortunate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:02.580
owners. A reverent tribute to director Dario&nbsp;
Argento and his film "Suspiria" in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:07.620
fans of this semi-forgotten era of Italian&nbsp;
horror will find a lot to love in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:12.180
film — especially the film's soundtrack&nbsp;
composed by the band Cavern of Anti-Matter.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:17.880
Strickland's three horror films so far have all&nbsp;
greatly impressed critics, but have failed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:23.280
connect successfully with general audiences in&nbsp;
the same way. "In Fabric" became Strickland's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:28.260
most critically acclaimed horror outing to date&nbsp;
when it was released in 2018, but it lacked the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:33.960
reach necessary to earn back its budget at the&nbsp;
box office. Despite having the largest budget of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:40.200
Strickland's career yet, it resulted in a measly&nbsp;
box office pull of less than $200K worldwide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:45.360
Perhaps "In Fabric" was simply a bit too&nbsp;
weird to resonate with the general public,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:51.120
but horror aficionados are bound to appreciate&nbsp;
the commitment to its throwback style.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:57.180
"Man of Tai Chi" is the first and so far&nbsp;
only feature film directed by everyone's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:03.480
favorite action star, Keanu Reeves. His love&nbsp;
for martial arts is abundantly apparent in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:08.640
remarkably authentic kung fu flick, which was&nbsp;
shot in China and has the bulk of its dialogue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:14.280
spoken in Mandarin and Cantonese. Reeves'&nbsp;
direction is full of stylistic throwbacks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:19.440
the kung fu films of days past. Reeves cast&nbsp;
himself as the film's main villain across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.420
from martial artist Tiger Hu Chen, whom Reeves&nbsp;
met during production on "The Matrix" series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:29.820
Reeves also brought along "Matrix" stunt&nbsp;
coordinator and legendary action director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:34.740
Yuen Woo-Ping, who ensured that "Man&nbsp;
of Tai Chi" had excellent fight scenes.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:41.280
"We wanna see a pure-hearted, good-natured&nbsp;
man of tai chi become a killer."

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:47.880
Given that Chinese was so prominent in the film,&nbsp;
Reeves' directorial debut was aimed more at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:52.500
Asian box office than the U.S. box office —&nbsp;
and his star power in both countries could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:57.960
have resulted in a high-grossing hit. Instead,&nbsp;
"Man of Tai Chi" unfortunately struggled to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:03.660
connect with audiences in either country. With&nbsp;
a budget of $32 million — which is quite high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:09.600
for a kung fu film — "Man of Tai Chi" made less&nbsp;
than $5 million in China and struggled to pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:15.540
in even $100K stateside. With his directorial&nbsp;
debut resulting in a massive financial failure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:18.960
Reeves has stuck to working in&nbsp;
front of the camera ever since,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:23.280
and he starred in the first "John&nbsp;
Wick" film the year after its release.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:28.800
"Spontaneous Combustion" is a largely&nbsp;
forgotten horror movie from 1989&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.420
that languishes in obscurity today.&nbsp;
Despite being a box office failure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:37.380
though, it just might be worthy of&nbsp;
a critical reevaluation nowadays.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:43.200
The plot of this film follows a walking nuclear&nbsp;
bomb played by Brad Dourif, who had lent his voice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.540
to the killer doll Chucky for the first time the&nbsp;
previous year. When Dourif's character gets angry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:53.940
flames erupt from his body and those around him —&nbsp;
so naturally, he embarks on a quest for answers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.940 --> 00:05:00.720
The plot is a tangled mess that doesn't hold up to&nbsp;
any amount of scrutiny, but it's still a schlocky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.340
fun time with an explosive lead performance&nbsp;
and incredible special effects sequences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:10.620
Director Tobe Hooper, more commonly known&nbsp;
for "The Texas Chain Saw Massacre" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:14.940
"Poltergeist," holds the film together&nbsp;
even as the script falls apart entirely.

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:21.660
"Spontaneous Combustion" was made for a budget&nbsp;
of $5.5 million, but had the insulting box office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:28.200
result of just $50K before it was pulled from&nbsp;
theaters and seemingly vanished from existence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:34.200
altogether. It was the latest in a string of box&nbsp;
office failures for Hooper, whose career was in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:39.720
dire place by the end of the '80s. The film can be&nbsp;
quite hard to find these days — it isn't available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:46.500
to stream or rent digitally, and physical versions&nbsp;
of it are pretty rare. Still, if a copy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:51.480
"Spontaneous Combustion" does work its way into&nbsp;
your life, you should definitely give it a shot.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:58.200
Released in 1980, "The Ninth Configuration" was&nbsp;
the directorial debut of William Peter Blatty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:02.820
the author and screenwriter of "The&nbsp;
Exorcist." Just like that earlier smash hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:08.580
"The Ninth Configuration" was adapted from one of&nbsp;
Blatty's novels — but unlike "The Exorcist," the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:13.320
film was a complete failure at the box office,&nbsp;
and has been condemned to relative obscurity.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:19.860
The story is set in an insane asylum run out of&nbsp;
a converted Gothic castle. Stacey Keach stars as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:24.480
Kane, a military psychiatrist who attempts&nbsp;
unorthodox therapy on the various inmates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:27.660
who all belong to branches of&nbsp;
U.S. government organizations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:33.780
Scott Wilson stars opposite Keach as an&nbsp;
astronaut who had a mental breakdown right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:39.360
before he was sent into space. What sounds like&nbsp;
the setup to another horror film is actually a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:44.520
darkly funny drama filled with thought-provoking&nbsp;
commentary on the nature of religion and humanity.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:49.200
"Who are you? You're too human to be human."

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:54.480
Warner Bros. was initially attached to distribute&nbsp;
the film, but after finding disastrous results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.480 --> 00:07:00.000
with a limited test release, the studio canceled&nbsp;
the planned nation-wide theatrical rollout.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.980
The scrapped distribution meant that the film's&nbsp;
box office earnings were virtually non-existent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:09.660
and Blatty's directorial debut never had&nbsp;
a real chance to connect with audiences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:16.320
Blatty only ever directed one other film — "The&nbsp;
Exorcist III," which is also both excellent and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.730
considered a financial disappointment,&nbsp;
much like "The Ninth Configuration. "

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:25.680
"Annihilation" is a thought-provoking sci-fi&nbsp;
adventure directed by Alex Garland of "Ex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:31.440
Machina" fame and adapted from the novel of&nbsp;
the same name by author Jeff VanderMeer. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:36.480
story concerns an exploratory rescue mission&nbsp;
into a zone overtaken by an alien substance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:42.180
dubbed The Shimmer, which changes the rules&nbsp;
of biology in all sorts of disturbing ways.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:47.400
Despite being an all-around excellent sci-fi&nbsp;
film, "Annihilation" had its chances of success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:52.740
hamstrung before it was even released. The&nbsp;
film was the unfortunate subject of one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:57.900
the most contentious and high-profile public&nbsp;
disputes between producers in recent years:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:04.140
David Ellison of Skydance Media wanted to dumb&nbsp;
down the movie after poor test screenings made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:09.420
him fear it was too smart for audiences. In&nbsp;
the end, Garland was able to resist the studio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:15.000
pressure and deliver on his intended vision, but&nbsp;
the film's distribution strategy was essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:19.800
sabotaged as a consequence. Planned theatrical&nbsp;
distribution was exchanged for streaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:24.600
premieres in most territories, which resulted in&nbsp;
an otherwise avoidable failure at the box office.

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:30.060
Writer-director Robert Eggers put himself&nbsp;
on the map with his 2015 feature-length&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:34.380
directorial debut "The Witch," a&nbsp;
slow-burn historical horror that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:40.320
turned a $3.5 million budget into $40&nbsp;
million of ticket sales. His next film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:45.840
"The Lighthouse," was made for a similarly small&nbsp;
budget, and once again managed to turn a profit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:52.020
Unfortunately, Eggers' third film,"The&nbsp;
Northman," ended that winning streak.

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:56.640
"The Northman" carries over the grungy&nbsp;
period-piece nature of Eggers' previous films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:02.640
while drastically increasing the scope of the&nbsp;
story and world. The film is a Viking epic with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:07.620
a flair for the supernatural that certainly has&nbsp;
far more mainstream appeal than "The Witch" or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:13.140
"The Lighthouse" — but it proved to still be a&nbsp;
bit too arthouse for the general public. Though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:18.000
it cost significantly more money to make, "The&nbsp;
Northman" produced a smaller box office return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:25.200
than Eggers' debut feature. It earned just shy&nbsp;
of $35 million at the domestic box office — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:30.300
huge disappointment for Universal Pictures, which&nbsp;
gave the production a massive $90 million budget.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:35.760
To make the situation even tougher, it&nbsp;
is believed that $200 million was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:39.180
true figure the film needed to hit in&nbsp;
order to avoid being a financial loss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:43.080
after the costs of marketing and&nbsp;
international distribution were squared away.

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:44.340
"It's a nightmare."

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:49.140
"Then you must wake up."

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:55.500
When Eggers spoke to The Daily Beast about the&nbsp;
film's poor box office performance, he said —

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:58.680
"I need to restrategize in terms&nbsp;
of what I'm pitching to a studio."

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:04.020
He added that he would continue to resist&nbsp;
taking the tentpole blockbuster route and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:08.220
stay true to himself one way or another,&nbsp;
in spite of concerns about marketability.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:13.920
"Maximum Overdrive" is an exceptionally&nbsp;
strange piece of work, not least of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:19.020
because it's the only movie ever directed by&nbsp;
author Stephen King. Dozens of films have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:24.240
adapted from King's writings, but this 1986&nbsp;
action-horror hybrid adapted from his short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:29.160
story "Trucks" is the only one he decided to&nbsp;
helm himself. He would later admit to being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:33.900
consistently high on cocaine throughout the&nbsp;
shoot, which undoubtedly factored into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:39.600
movie turning out as weird and messy as it&nbsp;
did. With a $10 million production budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:45.690
"Maximum Overdrive" only earned back just&nbsp;
shy of $7.5 million at the box office. King&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:51.420
has continued writing and occasionally producing,&nbsp;
but he's never tried his hand at directing again.

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:58.200
Despite all it’s critical derision, box-office&nbsp;
failure, and Razzie awards nominations for King&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:03.780
and star Emilio Estevez, "Maximum Overdrive" is&nbsp;
an exceptionally fun flick if you go in without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:09.540
expecting a masterpiece. The plot revolving&nbsp;
around machines coming to life is fast-paced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:14.040
and packed with creative death scenes,&nbsp;
demolition-derby-style vehicular carnage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:19.560
and too many massive explosions to count —&nbsp;
all scored by a rocking AC/DC soundtrack.

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:24.420
"The Master" is one of the best films from&nbsp;
acclaimed auteur Paul Thomas Anderson.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:29.460
Stunning cinematography, a challenging and&nbsp;
thought-provoking script, and two world-class&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:34.740
lead performances from Joaquin Phoenix and Philip&nbsp;
Seymour Hoffman culminate in a piece of work that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:40.560
is routinely hailed as one of the best films of&nbsp;
its decade. With such high prestige surrounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:46.680
"The Master," it's easy to forget that it was&nbsp;
considered a 2012 box-office bomb upon release.

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:49.980
"Leave your worries for a while, they'll&nbsp;
still be there when you get back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:53.760
And your memories aren't invited."

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:58.560
Concerns over the financial prospects of "The&nbsp;
Master" started mounting before the film even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:03.720
hit theaters. It was once meant to be handled&nbsp;
by Universal Pictures, but the studio dropped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:09.420
it out of fear that they wouldn't be able to turn&nbsp;
a profit on a $35 million budget. Universal wound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:14.040
up being even more right than they realized:&nbsp;
the film's final budget eventually ballooned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:19.620
all the way to $42 million, with the additional&nbsp;
costs mostly stemming from Anderson's decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:25.560
to shoot parts of his film in 65 millimeter and&nbsp;
exhibit the finished product in 70 millimeter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:31.740
The box office pull was only $16 million in&nbsp;
the U.S. after a somewhat fumbled expansion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:37.080
from limited release to wide release, and the&nbsp;
international box office only contributed another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:43.140
$11 million. That left the film significantly&nbsp;
short of making back its budget. Even with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:47.760
such overwhelming critical acclaim, Anderson&nbsp;
lost the funding that was in place for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:53.760
next film after the terrible box office results,&nbsp;
meaning he had to look elsewhere for financing.

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:59.040
"King Richard" wasn't the first sports biopic&nbsp;
to get Will Smith nominated for an Oscar. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:03.960
would be Michael Mann's "Ali," an excellently&nbsp;
acted film starring the former Fresh Prince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:10.800
as the greatest boxer who ever lived. Despite&nbsp;
the 2001 movie's critical acclaim and Smith's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:15.600
unforgettable performance as Muhammad Ali,&nbsp;
it still didn't manage to turn a profit.

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:22.560
"Ali" didn't actually perform particularly poorly&nbsp;
at the domestic or international box offices. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:27.900
brought in almost $90 million worldwide, which&nbsp;
would have been considered a success if "Ali"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:33.540
hadn't been so exorbitantly expensive. The film&nbsp;
went into production with an already overinflated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:40.320
budget of $107 million, but Mann ended up going&nbsp;
significantly over budget even before making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:46.620
the $16 million decision to extend the shoot and&nbsp;
relocate filming to Africa. To make matters worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:51.420
Sony pumped even more marketing dollars behind&nbsp;
the film than usual to try to make it a hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:56.340
but they just ended up digging themselves&nbsp;
into a deeper financial hole. It is believed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.340 --> 00:14:01.560
that Sony's total losses on the film surpassed&nbsp;
$100 million by the time all was said and done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:07.260
making "Ali" one of the all-time biggest&nbsp;
box-office disasters of this century.

